  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Punctate Masked Bee (Hylaeus punctatus) is a small solitary bee belonging to the Colletidae family, commonly known as plasterer bees due to their use of plant oils and resins to line their nests. This species is found throughout New England and plays an important role in pollinating native wildflowers and plants.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Facial Markings",
      "description": "Yellow or cream-colored facial marks on the head, particularly around the eyes and clypeus, giving the appearance of a 'mask'",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "Tiny bee, typically 4-6 mm in length, making it one of the smaller native bee species in New England",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Body Coloration",
      "description": "Predominantly black body with sparse punctate (dotted) texture and minimal body hair compared to other bee families",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Punctate Masked Bees are found in open woodlands, gardens, meadows, and sunny disturbed areas throughout New England where flowering plants are abundant.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike honeybees, these solitary bees use secretions from their abdomen to waterproof their nest cells, creating a smooth, cellophane-like lining—a behavior that inspired their family name 'plasterer bees.'",
